    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a> broke the Later Yan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-canhe-slope">Battle of Canhe Slope</a> in 395 and his successors conquered the Helian Xia, Northern Yan and, in 439, the Northern Liang, uniting the north and ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a>'s reforms of 485–486, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-field-system">equal-field</a> landholding system and the three-elders system, redistributed land against tax and labour obligations and registered the population, doubling the registered households to more than five million; the equal-field system lasted until the mid-8th century.</li><li>Emperor Xiaowen moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 494, changed the imperial surname to Yuan, encouraged marriage with Chinese families and abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> customs; the rift between this sinicised court and the steppe soldiers of the Six Garrisons brought the rebellions of 523 and the dynasty's collapse.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Northern Wei empress was chosen not by birth or imperial favour but by a ceremony in which the candidate had to forge a golden statue with her own hands; only a consort whose statue came out well could become empress.</p></aside>
    </section>

