    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei.jpg" alt="Emperor Daowu of Northern Wei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Emperor Daowu of Northern Wei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%91%E5%86%88%E7%9F%B3%E7%AA%9F03--CNSX-352-001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TAOZIlovewiki, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a>, or Tabgach, were a clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> confederation, whose state modern historians see as a synthesis of Chinese and nomadic traditions that rapidly sinicised. The Jin dynasty allied with them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> state of Han-Zhao, and in 315 the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba-yilu">Tuoba Yilu</a> was made Prince of Dai; Dai stagnated after him and fell to Former Qin in 376. When Fu Jiān's bid to unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> broke at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Battle of Fei River</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a>, son or grandson of the last Prince of Dai, reasserted Tuoba independence in 386, first as Prince of Dai and then as Prince of Wei, whence the state's name.</p><p>The young state leaned on the stronger Later Yan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong Xianbei</a> while subjugating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiefu">Tiefu</a> and Rouran, until in 391 Tuoba Gui refused further tribute after the Murong detained his brother. In 395 the Yan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> sent his crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-bao">Murong Bao</a> with a massive punitive army, and Tuoba Gui crushed it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-canhe-slope">Battle of Canhe Slope</a>. When he invaded in turn, Murong Bao's decision to hold only his capital and major cities let the Wei overrun the Central Plains, and the Battle of Baisi drove Later Yan back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a>. In 398 Tuoba Gui moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a>, and in 399 he took the title Emperor of Wei. To break the autonomy of the tribes he reorganised his people into eight artificial tribes settled around the capital as military units and removed their traditional leaders, making them the emperor's own professional military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Northern Wei began as a steppe polity ruling a Chinese countryside. Its officials drew no salaries until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a>'s time, and conquered populations were deported wholesale, some 460,000 to the capital under Emperor Daowu. Local society was ruled by military clientage: Han commoners served aristocratic magnates in fortified villages as armed retainers (buqu), and the Wei emperors made such magnates governors of their own fiefs. Nobles and generals received bondservants from war captives, who came to form much of the farming population, and an attempt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> free Han subjects in 473 only drove more into the magnates' clientage, leaving the state short of soldiers and taxes.</p><p>Empress Dowager Feng's reforms of 485–486 answered this. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-field-system">equal-field</a> system (juntian zhi) redistributed uncultivated land to commoners against obligations of grain, cloth and labour: open land for each adult male and half as much for each female, with inheritable mulberry or hemp land for textiles and sale forbidden. The three-elders system (sanzhang zhi) of 486 grouped households into neighbourhoods, villages and wards under appointed elders, to register the population and levy taxes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a>. The magnates' shelter from taxation was broken and the registered population doubled to more than five million households. Trade grew with conquest: in 439 the Wei carried the Sogdian merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>, and caravans came from Khotan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tongwan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongwan.jpg" alt="Tongwan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%BB%9F%E4%B8%87%E5%9F%8E.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cong, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> army was a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> horsemen settled around the capital, and it conquered the north. Emperor Taiwu, enthroned in 423, attacked the Tiefu-led Helian Xia in 426 and took its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwancheng</a> in 427; the Northern Liang in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> submitted as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, the Northern Yan fell in 436, and in 439 Taiwu conquered the Northern Liang outright, uniting the north and closing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period. The northern frontier was the Rouran, former subjects who built a khaganate on the steppe by 402; their raids were constant, and in 423 a wall over 2,000 li (1,080 kilometres) was built from Chicheng in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> to Wuyuan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> since the Han, and a second in 446.</p><p>Against the south the Wei fought the Liu Song, Southern Qi and Liang in turn. In 422–423 Emperor Mingyuan took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, Xuchang and Hulao and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>; Emperor Wen's great invasion of 450 was lured across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, flanked and destroyed, and Wei armies laid waste the provinces to the south before withdrawing in January 451. In 466–467 the Song governors of Xu and Yan provinces surrendered the populous lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a> to the Wei. War with the Southern Qi from 479 achieved little before the peace of 490. Against the Liang the Wei took Xinyang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a> by 505, but Wei Rui took Hefei in 506 and with Cao Jingzong destroyed the Wei at the Battle of Zhongli, ending large-scale war for years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xianbei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xianbei.jpg" alt="Xianbei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MurongPainting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown tomb painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Wei court kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> customs that struck Chinese observers as strange: empresses chosen by forging a golden statue, a single braid worn by every man, and the rule that the mother of a newly named crown prince must kill herself, so that emperors, motherless, honoured their wet nurses as 'Nurse Empress Dowager'. Cui Hao (381–450), adviser at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a> court, introduced Han administrative methods and penal codes and built a Taoist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocracy">theocracy</a> that lasted until 450. The emperors claimed descent from the Yellow Emperor, sacrificed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and enfeoffed his descendants in 472 and 495.</p><p>The great reformers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a> (438–490), regent for her grandson Emperor Xiaowen, who brought Han aristocrats into government and in 485–486 built the fiscal structure of a centralised Chinese-style empire, and Xiaowen himself. In 494 he moved the capital from Pingcheng to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and made sinicisation systematic: Xianbei traditions were largely abandoned, the imperial family took the surname Yuan, marriage with Chinese families was encouraged, and the Xianbei noble clans took Chinese names. Buddhist temples rose everywhere and displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> as the state religion. The weakness lay in regency: Emperor Xiaoming inherited as an infant, Empress Dowager Hu ruled in his name for over a decade and poisoned him in 528, and thereafter emperors were puppets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzhu-rong">Erzhu Rong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, chosen, deposed and executed at will.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes.jpg" alt="Yungang Grottoes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes">Yungang Grottoes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:61292-Yungang-Grottoes_(28498548881).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Northern Wei was the longest-lived and most powerful of the Northern dynasties, and historians generally credit it with laying the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s reunification under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in 589. Its reforms reversed the devolution of power to local strongmen; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-field-system">equal-field</a> system outlived the dynasty to the mid-8th century, and Ray Huang has called these reforms the 'infrastructure of a regenerated empire' whose agricultural resources and peasant manpower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Yang Jian</a> inherited to unite China. Chen Yinke saw the institutional and aristocratic origins of the Sui and Tang as rooted in the multi-ethnic legacy of the Northern dynasties, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> Wei above all.</p><p>Under the Wei <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, a foreign idea, became firmly established in China. The emperors converted and became great patrons of Buddhist art, influenced through the trade routes by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes">Yungang Grottoes</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a> were cut in the mid-to-late 5th century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longmen-grottoes">Longmen Grottoes</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> towards the dynasty's end, holding more than 30,000 Buddhist images of the period, and the Shaolin Monastery and the Songyue Pagoda were Wei foundations. The age produced the Qimin Yaoshu on agriculture, the Commentary on the Water Classic, a monumental geography, and the legend of Hua Mulan. The ethnic integration pursued under Xiaowen made the Northern Wei the model of the 'Northern Dynasty' synthesis of steppe and China on which the Tang was built.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui.jpg" alt="Emperor Wen of Sui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Emperor Wen of Sui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sui_Wendi_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yan Li-pen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dynasty broke along the line its success had drawn, between a governing aristocracy that had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and adopted Han-style sedentary life and the nomadic tribal armies of the Six Garrisons on the northern frontier. In 523, when drought and famine drove the Rouran to raid in April, the people of a frontier town killed their commander and rebellion spread: Poliuhan Baling took Woye, Mozhe Dati rose in Qinzhou, Hu Chen made himself King of Gaoping and Ge Rong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> proclaimed himself Emperor of Qi; Baling fell in 525, but the risings lasted until 530.</p><p>At court Empress Dowager Hu, who had ruled for more than ten years for the infant Emperor Xiaoming, poisoned him in 528 as he reached eighteen and prepared to take power. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzhu-rong">Erzhu Rong</a> summoned the officials of Luoyang and butchered them, some 2,000 courtiers, throwing the empress dowager and her child puppet Yuan Zhao into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>: the Heyin massacre of 528. His emperor, Xiaozhuang, killed Erzhu Rong in a palace ambush in 530, only to be overthrown by the Erzhu clan in 531. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> raised Han and non-Han troops against the clan, took Luoyang in 532, deposed two emperors and enthroned Xiaowu, who fled west to the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>. In 534–535 the empire split into Eastern Wei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> under the same house, and in 550 and 557 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> took their places, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Yang Jian</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in 581.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Northern Wei, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a> in 386, unified northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 439 and ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-field-system">equal-field</a> reforms, Emperor Xiaowen's sinicisation and the grottoes of Yungang and Longmen outlasted the dynasty, which split in 534–535 after the Six Garrisons rebelled and the Heyin massacre destroyed its court.</em></p>

